From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 15:34:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: Keep Application Restart and Recovery and the WER
 registrations as Windows 11 keeps them, and act on them when the process
 crashes.

What a process registers lives in the block PEB->WerRegistrationData points to, laid out as on Windows 11 x64
(0x968 bytes, "PEB_SIGNATURE", the restart flags at 0x20 and command line at 0xa8, the recovery callback at
0x8b0, the WER flags at 0x914), so GetApplicationRestartSettings, GetApplicationRecoveryCallback and WerGetFlags
read it from another process with PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION | PROCESS_VM_READ, as Windows does.

As measured on Windows 11 build 29671:
- a restart command line of 1024 characters or more is E_INVALIDARG; asking with no buffer gives the size, a
  buffer too small comes back empty with ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER, and after unregistering it is ERROR_NOT_FOUND
  with a size of 0;
- a recovery ping interval of 0 is 5000 ms and more than 300000 E_INVALIDARG; after unregistering the callback
  reads back as zeros, before any registration as ERROR_NOT_FOUND;
- WER files and memory blocks share 512 places, file names match whatever their case, memory blocks are up to
  64 KiB; metadata keys start with a letter and go on in [A-Za-z0-9._-], values are under 128 characters, and
  there are 8 of them; runtime exception modules are 16, told apart by path and context; any flags are kept.

When the process crashes, before the debugger is started, the recovery callback runs on its own thread for as long
as each ApplicationRecoveryInProgress buys it another ping interval, and the process is restarted with its
registered command line if it asked to be, not with RESTART_NO_CRASH, and has run for a minute.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

